As an interface (with a default method) — no shared state, so no abstract class needed:

```java
interface Notifier {
    void send(String message);
    default void sendUrgent(String message) {
        send("URGENT: " + message);
    }
}

class EmailNotifier implements Notifier {
    public void send(String message) { /* send email */ }
}
```

As an abstract class — because now every notifier shares real state (a retry count) and shared logic that uses it:

```java
abstract class BaseNotifier {
    protected int retryCount = 0;
    void sendWithRetry(String message) {
        retryCount++;
        send(message);
    }
    abstract void send(String message);
}

class EmailNotifier extends BaseNotifier {
    void send(String message) { /* send email */ }
}
```

The deciding factor was `retryCount` — real shared state — which pushed the second version toward an abstract class; the first version needed no state at all, so an interface was the leaner fit.
